WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.551 --> 00:00:07.840
Speaker 1: And hey, speaking of people who&nbsp;
didn't do well in polls, Madison Cawthorn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.120
has been back in the news pretty much for&nbsp;
about a week or so now because the guy just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:20.080
can't stop having problems with his campaign&nbsp;
finances. And I know that seems weird, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.000
Because the guy lost his primary. Obviously,&nbsp;
he's not running for office anymore. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.840
he's a lame duck. He's going to be leaving at the&nbsp;
end of the year. But last week, it was revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:38.640
that Madison Cawthorn is a month late. In filing&nbsp;
his most recent campaign finance disclosures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.029
We also know that in those disclosures.
Speaker 2: That are still waiting to be.&nbsp;

00:00:45.029 --> 00:00:48.800
Speaker 1: Filed, he's got thousands of&nbsp;
dollars that he's spent at Chick-Fil-A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:56.240
thousands of dollars that he spent at cigar shops.&nbsp;
And what some of the other 21,000 for lodging in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:04.389
Florida, $3,000 at a place called Papa's Beer. And&nbsp;
of course, thousands, hundreds of thousands of.&nbsp;

00:01:04.389 --> 00:01:08.240
Speaker 2: Dollars in consulting fees&nbsp;
that he's paid out to his consultants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.240
who clearly did not do a very good job for him.
Speaker 1: But he's a month late and filed it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:21.200
And now, according to a new report from The Daily&nbsp;
Beast, we know why. Because as it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:27.120
according to this new report, Madison Cawthorn is&nbsp;
out of money. The people working for his campaign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.480
said it was basically dollar in, dollar out. Guy&nbsp;
raised millions, but he spent even more than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.520
Now, you may not think that's a problem,&nbsp;
right? Ha, ha. You ran out of money.&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.000
Speaker 2: You're not going to be&nbsp;
reelected anyway, so you suck. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.400
a little bit murkier than that, though, folks.
Speaker 1: You see, there's a law. Believe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.920
or not, we actually do still have a couple.
Speaker 2: Campaign finance laws out there.&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.040
Speaker 1: And one of them says that you can&nbsp;
raise money for both your primary race and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:02.080
the general election race at the same&nbsp;
time. Good. However, you cannot touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:09.840
that general election money until you've won&nbsp;
your primary. And if you don't win your primary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.160
you have to pay all that money back to the.
Speaker 2: Donors who gave it to you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.480
The General Election Fund.
Speaker 1: Doesn't matter what happens with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.600
primary money. If you lose, you lose whatever.&nbsp;
But if you've raised money for a general election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.440
and you lose your primary, all of the general&nbsp;
election money by law has to go back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.360
people that gave it to you. And that makes sense,&nbsp;
right? That's actually protecting the donors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.520
Which explains why we have that law, because.
Speaker 2: It protects the wealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.120
corporations pouring.
Speaker 1: Money in Madison. Cawthorn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.160
as I said, is out of money. And he had a&nbsp;
lot of money in his general election fund.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.040
So put two and two together and you realize that&nbsp;
Madison Cawthorn can't afford to pay back all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.520
money that he is supposed to be paying back.&nbsp;
And that is according to the people who worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:03.840
with his campaign that spoke to The Daily Beast.&nbsp;
So Madison Cawthorn is in a lot of trouble here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.400
He's going to have to come up&nbsp;
possibly with millions of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.440
to return to his donors and that likely. Again,&nbsp;
we can't say for certain, but it is likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.080
why he hasn't gone ahead and filed his campaign&nbsp;
finance disclosures, because it would show that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.960
he spent all the money that he's legally.
Speaker 2: Not allowed to spend.&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.960
Speaker 1: So in addition to&nbsp;
no longer being in Congress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.880
he may no longer be a free man if he continues&nbsp;
to spend money so frivolously that he's legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.200
not allowed to touch if he&nbsp;
does have any money left over.&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.480
Speaker 2: I'd say it's time&nbsp;
to spend that on a good lawyer.

